WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:40.610
As I've mentioned to you before each of the
3 aspects of experience so the things you

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:48.040
know the things you don’t know and the fact
of yourself have a very ambivalent underlying

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.950
structure both positive and negative so that
the things you don’t know are interesting

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:58.710
and compelling in the sources of new information
but also the source of the things that undermine

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:03.710
you both physically and mentally
And the things you know of course your culture

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:13.730
disciplines you and shapes you into a full
person but also at exactly the same time molds

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:18.710
you and crushes you in a particular direction
rather than any of the directions you might

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.520
have gone in
And then finally with regards to the individual

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.600
we spent a substantial amount of time discussing
the individual’s capability capacity to

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.820
make order out of chaos and sometimes to make
chaos out of order in the service of a higher

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.929
order
And that’s all to the good but just like

00:01:38.929 --> 00:01:43.250
culture and nature have their negative aspects
so do the individual

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:50.929
And I think personally this is where Christian
mythology in particular comes into its own

00:01:50.929 --> 00:01:58.820
I think of all the major religions Christianity
has the most thoroughly developed what you

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:04.850
might describe as formal model of evil and
that model isn't part of the canonical writings

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:11.039
of Christianity say encapsulated in the Bible
but part of cloud of sort of natural mythology

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:14.110
and storytelling that surrounds the canonical
writings

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:20.069
so you could say that although Christainity
and Buddhism have spent a substantial amount

00:02:20.069 --> 00:02:26.930
of time developing up a representation of
the hero Christianity in particular has also

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:33.330
spent a substantial amount of time developing
a formal portrait of the figure that stands

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.830
in opposition to the hero
and I think the most appropriate term for

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:45.269
that figure who takes multiple forms in mythology
is the adversary because the adversarial spirit

00:02:45.269 --> 00:02:53.709
is a spirit that stands in opposition to everything
Stands in opposition to nature stands in opposition

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:59.069
to culture and most specifically stands in
opposition to that aspect of the human being

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:05.819
that’s both exploratory and creative
Now the last time we talked I had a chance

00:03:05.819 --> 00:03:13.150
to describe to you how the figures of the
adversarial brothers emerged naturally at

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:18.450
the end of genesis as a coda to the story
right Adam and Eve develop self consciousness

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:23.909
they develop knowledge of their own mortality
and death and as the primordial parents of

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:30.299
humanity their first children take the form
of the hostile brothers which is to say that

00:03:30.299 --> 00:03:36.590
if you're the child of nature the child of
culture this sort of ultimate parents then

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:40.590
as an individual you take two forms a positive
form and a negative form

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:45.790
and the negative form is characterized in
many ways by the kind of absolute hatred of

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:53.379
the good hatred of the positive form and I
think that you can’t understand the full

00:03:53.379 --> 00:04:00.989
human propensity for evil without considering
more than the territoriality more than the

00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:04.579
innate territoriality of human being
so if you look at animals well animals are

00:04:04.579 --> 00:04:10.540
territorial and they fight to preserve their
territory and it’s a rational struggle they're

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.819
fighting for resources and for a place that
they can that they can operate and live in

00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:20.200
and reproduce in efficiently
but human beings are substantively different

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:26.840
from that in that their agonistic conflict
their aggression often seems to be motivated

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.090
by something more akin to the pure desire
for destruction rather than for any rational

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:37.360
end whatsoever
And so from that perspective I would say as

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:44.310
an example the fact that Hitler ended up committing
suicide in a bunker beneath Germany’s capital

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:48.460
at the end of the second world war when Berlin
was in flames when all of Germany was in flames

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:52.210
and when his country was completely defeated
after tens of millions of people had died

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:56.740
in the conflict including of course the 7
million or so people that were killed in the

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:57.910
Holocaust.

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:03.270
the normal mode of interpretation of that
would be what a terrible defeat for Hitler

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:09.490
but an equally valid and I think um an equally
valid prima facie argument and one I think

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:14.340
that’s actually more valid is that wasn’t
a defeat for Hitler at all it was precisely

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:21.110
what he was aiming at right from the beginning
because his mode of being was intensely adversarial

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:25.990
And I would also say that it’s certainly
possible that the full nature of these motivations

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:31.770
weren't even necessarily clear to him As they
unfolded across time during the second world

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:36.430
war no more than the full motivations of any
human being are necessarily accessible to

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:39.060
them as they act out whatever it is that they
act out

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:44.919
so Carl Jung says for example that we act
out great mythological stories that doesn’t

00:05:44.919 --> 00:05:48.710
necessarily mean you know the story just means
you act it out

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:54.020
so for example you see people whose lives
are repetitive bouts of tragedy and what they're

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:59.300
acting out is a tragic story and they know
it insofar as they're actually acting it out

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:04.270
but they don’t necessarily have an explicit
model of the relationship between their patterns

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:07.050
of behaviour and the constantly tragic outcome
they produce

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:11.729
Well that doesn’t even necessarily mean
that what they're doing isn't voluntary Because

00:06:11.729 --> 00:06:16.449
things can be voluntary even if you don’t
understand them fully

00:06:16.449 --> 00:06:20.979
There's an aspect to the human psyche that’s
a “nest of vipers” so to speak because

00:06:20.979 --> 00:06:24.879
you can’t necessarily trust what you see
and that means not only when you're looking

00:06:24.879 --> 00:06:28.419
at someone else say but even when you're looking
in the mirror you can’t be sure that what

00:06:28.419 --> 00:06:31.639
you say you're doing is exactly what you're
doing.

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:39.659
Your motivations aren't transparent and they
may not even be clear

00:06:39.659 --> 00:06:45.280
so I suppose the idea that lurks behind this
formalization is that freedom of choice is

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:53.460
such a good that one of its subsidiary necessities
which is that there has to be a polar distinction

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:59.069
between good and evil is worth having freedom
is so important that it justifies the distinction

00:06:59.069 --> 00:07:03.240
between good and evil
and I think that art I think that’s a reasonable

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:11.000
way to presume It’s reasonable to presume
that that’s the manner in which experience

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.880
is actually structured and there's complex
reasons for that

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:19.849
one would be that under the most optimal circumstances
which is something we’ll talk about as this

00:07:19.849 --> 00:07:24.349
proceeds to a close that means in a sense
you can have your cake and eat it too

00:07:24.349 --> 00:07:29.800
Which means the potential existence of good
and evil allows for freedom of choice And

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:34.620
then if the choice is always towards the good
then you have the benefits of freedom of choice

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:38.949
plus the benefits of the good the only price
you have to pay is the constant possibility

00:07:38.949 --> 00:07:39.949
of evil.

00:07:39.949 --> 00:07:46.240
It’s very much like the structure of Christianity
so you have the highest god who’s whose

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.629
highest son is Christ but you have the figure
of the Satan lurking in the background who’s

00:07:50.629 --> 00:07:54.259
also got a filial relationship with the highest
god.

00:07:54.259 --> 00:07:58.760
and it makes for a confusing kind of theology
because in many situations of course Christ

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:04.419
is identified specifically with God the father
